---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { population, closedCount, cohortSize, model } from "../lib/data.js";

const steps = [
  ["question", "The question"],
  ["cutoff", "One date splits past from future"],
  ["leakage", "The leak we found"],
  ["news", "Collecting the news"],
  ["matching", "Is this headline about them?"],
  ["scoring", "Reading the tone"],
  ["features", "From headlines to features"],
  ["models", "The models"],
  ["evaluation", "Testing it honestly"],
  ["platform", "The live platform"],
  ["limits", "What it can't do"],
];

const timeline = `founded ──── first round ──── ░░░░ 2 years of news ░░░░ ┃ cutoff ┃ ──── outcome
                                                          ┃        ┃
                     everything left of the line is evidence, right of it the answer`;
---

<Base title="How it works · Survivorship" description="How the startup survival pipeline works, from the 2013 cutoff to the live watchlist." page="how">
  <section style="padding-bottom: 20px">
    <div class="head center" style="margin: 0 auto">
      <h1 class="h1">How it works</h1>
      <p class="lede">A distributed pipeline in Scala and Apache Spark, trained on a Crunchbase snapshot and served live by a small Scala server. One step at a time.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="longform" style="padding-top: 40px">
    <ol class="toc" aria-label="Steps">
      {steps.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}
    </ol>

    <div>
      <article class="step" id="question">
        <h2>The question</h2>
        <p>
          Investors read the news. Does what the press says about a young company tell you anything about whether it survives,
          beyond what its age, funding history, sector and country already tell you? Everything here exists to answer that one
          question without fooling ourselves.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="step" id="cutoff">
        <h2>One date splits past from future</h2>
        <p>
          Every startup is judged as of <strong>1 January 2013</strong>. Features may only use what was knowable before then; the
          label is what happened after. Of the {population.toLocaleString("en-US")} startups founded and funded before the cutoff,
          {" "}{closedCount.toLocaleString("en-US")} ({((closedCount / population) * 100).toFixed(1)}%) had closed by the 2013–2014 snapshot.
          Acquired companies are left out, because an acquisition is neither a clean success nor a failure.
        </p>
        <pre class="formula" aria-label="Timeline: founded, first round, two years of news, cutoff, outcome">{timeline}</pre>
      </article>

      <article class="step" id="leakage">
        <h2>The leak we found</h2>
        <p>
          Crunchbase's funding totals are lifetime figures from 2014, so they include rounds raised after the cutoff. Our first
          version only used totals when the last round came before 2013, and recorded whether that guard passed as a flag. The flag
          itself leaked: it was false almost exactly when a company raised again after 2013, which means it was still alive.
        </p>
        <p>
          Removing everything that depends on lifetime funding cost 0.044 AUC. The leak-free model keeps only facts fixed before
          the cutoff: age, years since the first round, the gap from founding to that round, the top {model.categories.length}
          {" "}categories and the top {model.countries.length} countries.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="step" id="news">
        <h2>Collecting the news</h2>
        <p>
          Free news APIs don't reach back to 2011, so <code>BatchNewsCollector</code> queries Google News RSS with a date window
          for each company. It is resumable, gentle with rate limits, and works through a balanced cohort of {cohortSize} startups,
          half that closed and half that didn't, so coverage can't simply track survival. Raw headlines are stored before matching,
          so the matcher can change without collecting again.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="step" id="matching">
        <h2>Is this headline about them?</h2>
        <p>
          A search for "Kabbage" returns an article about tomato barons. <code>NameMatcher</code> normalises both texts, drops
          suffixes like Inc and Ltd, and gives each headline a confidence. Only 0.6 and above is used.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>1.0</strong> when the full name appears and at least one of its words is distinctive.</li>
          <li><strong>0.6</strong> when every word of the name is an everyday word, like "Wireless Tech".</li>
          <li><strong>0.3</strong> when the name is one short or everyday word, like Ramp or Notion, so it is set aside.</li>
        </ul>
        <form class="card try" id="match-try">
          <div class="row-inputs">
            <div class="field"><label for="m-name">Company</label><input id="m-name" name="name" value="Kabbage" maxlength="60" required /></div>
            <span></span>
          </div>
          <div class="row-inputs" style="margin-top: 12px">
            <div class="field"><label for="m-headline">Headline</label><input id="m-headline" name="headline" value="The tomato barons of the occupied Western Sahara" maxlength="300" required /></div>
            <button>Check</button>
          </div>
          <p class="result" aria-live="polite"></p>
          <p class="error" role="alert"></p>
        </form>
        <p>
          A hand audit of 100 accepted headlines found 29 strictly about the company and 38 about its product. Matching is the
          weakest link in the pipeline.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="step" id="scoring">
        <h2>Reading the tone</h2>
        <p><code>SentimentScorer</code> uses a small finance-and-startup lexicon, so every score can be explained word by word.</p>
        <pre class="formula">score = positive words − negative words</pre>
        <form class="card try" id="tone-try">
          <div class="row-inputs">
            <div class="field"><label for="s-headline">Headline</label><input id="s-headline" name="headline" value="Startup X raises major funding and expands into new markets" maxlength="300" required /></div>
            <button>Score</button>
          </div>
          <p class="result tokens" aria-live="polite"></p>
          <p class="error" role="alert"></p>
        </form>
        <p>There is no negation handling, so "can't buy success" still counts as positive.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="step" id="features">
        <h2>From headlines to features</h2>
        <p>Spark groups each startup's scored headlines and computes, from news before the cutoff only:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Coverage: how many headlines there were, and whether there were any.</li>
          <li>Tone: shares of positive, negative and neutral headlines, the average, the most recent, the trend from older to newer, and how much it swings.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>A <code>leftOuterJoin</code> keeps every startup, so a company nobody wrote about gets a neutral baseline instead of vanishing.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="step" id="models">
        <h2>The models</h2>
        <p>
          Spark MLlib logistic regression and a linear SVM. Only 13% of startups closed, so an unweighted model learns to say
          "operating" for everyone. Balanced class weights, computed from training rows only, make both outcomes count equally.
        </p>
        <pre class="formula">weight(class) = rows ÷ (2 × rows in that class)
P(keeps going) = 1 ÷ (1 + e^−(b + w·x))</pre>
      </article>

      <article class="step" id="evaluation">
        <h2>Testing it honestly</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Stratified five-fold cross-validation, with the same folds for every model, so comparisons are paired.</li>
          <li>Decision thresholds are tuned inside each training fold, never on the fold being tested.</li>
          <li>A paired bootstrap with 2,000 resamples puts a 95% interval on every difference.</li>
          <li>Feature sets are nested (leak-free, then plus coverage, then plus tone), so each step isolates one idea.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>News volume adds nothing; news tone adds about 0.01 AUC, which doesn't survive a correction for multiple tests. <a href="/#answer">See the numbers.</a></p>
      </article>

      <article class="step" id="platform">
        <h2>The live platform</h2>
        <p><code>web/Server.scala</code> serves this site and a small API backed by the same Scala code, in one Docker image on Render.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Scoring</strong> on the overview page runs the leak-free model with today as the cutoff.</li>
          <li><strong>The watchlist</strong> is re-checked every six hours: the last two years of Google News for each startup, matched, scored and run through both models. A GitHub Actions cron job wakes the free server so the schedule holds.</li>
          <li><strong>Your startup</strong> is scored immediately and saved in a Render Key Value store, so it stays on the watchlist across restarts.</li>
          <li><strong>Everyday-word names</strong> like Ramp get help on the live pages only: their news search is narrowed with a founder or product name, and a headline must spell the name exactly as the company does.</li>
          <li><strong>Established companies</strong> get a gentle warning. The model's weight on years since the first round is negative, so for a company long past its first round the score says more about age than about survival.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="step" id="limits">
        <h2>What it can't do</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>It learned from startups judged before 2013. Today's companies raise and fail on different timelines.</li>
          <li>The tone weights come from only 69 startups with matched headlines, so the news score is illustrative.</li>
          <li>An AUC of 0.68 ranks startups better than chance, but it is far from a verdict on any single company.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><a href="/startups/">See it on real startups.</a></p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script>import "../scripts/how.js";</script>
</Base>
